.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_SET_CONTEXT 3 "2026-10-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_set_context \- attach a value to a request for later middleware and the handler
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_req_set_context(chttpsvr_req *req,
                                       const void *key, void *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attaches value to req under key.  Every later step of the request's
chain (the middleware after this one, and the handler) reads it back
with chttpsvr_req_context.  This is where a middleware that
authenticates a request, or gives it a trace identifier, stores what
it found.

Each request starts with an empty set of values of its own, so
nothing that one request stores reaches another request, even on the
same connection.

Keys are compared by address and nothing else, as Go compares the
keys of context.WithValue by identity.  The usual key is the address
of a static object in the module that stores the value, so that two
independent middleware never share a key by accident.  A second call
with a key the request already holds replaces the value.  A NULL
value is stored like any other.

The values stay attached until the chain of the request returns.  For
a matched route that is when the handler and every middleware around
it have returned, so a middleware can still read them after its call
to next returns.  For a rejected request that runs its middleware (a
404, 405 or 413; see chttpsvr_use(3)) it is when the outermost
middleware returns.  After that the library forgets them.  It never
frees, dereferences or copies a value.

The first value costs no allocation.  Further values use the
allocator of the server, and their storage is released when the
chain returns.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req     The request.
key     The key, compared by address.  It must not be NULL.
value   The value to attach, which may be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            The value is attached.
ccol_invalid_args       req or key is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory  The storage for the value could not be
                        allocated.  The request keeps the values that
                        it had.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library never frees a value, so a value that points to memory a
middleware allocated has to be freed by that middleware, for example
after its call to next returns.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
A request belongs to one thread at a time, the one that runs its
chain: a worker thread for a buffered route, and a streaming thread
for a streaming route.  Call this function only on that thread and
only while the chain runs.  It takes no lock.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
static const char user_key; /* its address is the key */

static void auth_mw(chttpsvr_req *req, chttpsvr_resp *resp, void *ctx,
                    chttpsvr_next_fn next) {
  user_t *user = lookup_user(ctx, chttpsvr_req_header(req, "authorization"));
  if (!user) {
    chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, 401);
    return;
  }
  if (chttpsvr_req_set_context(req, &user_key, user) != ccol_success) {
    release_user(user);
    chttpsvr_resp_set_status(resp, 500);
    return;
  }
  next(req, resp);
  release_user(user);
}

static void profile_handler(chttpsvr_req *req, chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                            void *ctx) {
  (void)ctx;
  user_t *user = chttpsvr_req_context(req, &user_key);
  chttpsvr_resp_printf(resp, "hello %s", user->name);
}
.fi
